    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d415">

                <pb facs="0415" n="405" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg405"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/921</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by the Adviser on
                        Political Relations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-04-29">April 29, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Admiral Leahy<anchor n="10" xml:id="d415-f1"/> telephoned me this morning and
                    said that he had taken up with the President the note we had from the British
                    Embassy informing us of Prime Minister Churchill’s statement on the possible use
                    of poison gas by the Germans. Admiral Leahy said that the President felt that in
                    view of the Prime Minister’s statement it would serve no useful purpose for the
                    President to take any action along these lines at present.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James Clement Dunn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d415-f1" n="10" xml:id="d415-f1-content">
                <p>Adm. William D. Leahy, Chief of Staff to the Commander in Chief of the Army and
                    Navy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
